<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dur-Kurigalzu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Aqar Quf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dur-Kurigalzu was a royal city founded by the Kassite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i">Kurigalzu I</a> in the early 14th century BCE, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It contained palaces and temples and a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, whose mud-brick core still stands at Aqar Quf. It remained an important centre of Kassite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> until the dynasty fell in the 12th century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dur-kurigalzu.jpg" alt="Dur-Kurigalzu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1._The_Kassite_era_ziggurat_at_Aqar-Quf_(Dur-Kurigalzu),_western_Baghdad,_Iraq._December_29,_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
